{"highest":3,"ids":{"red-team lens, medium: When the POST response has no parseable `created_at` (jq falls back to epoch 0, so `_CCGL_RACE_NOTE_AT=0`), the guard is false and `since` stays 0, so every historical concurrent claim note on the issue is included in the read-back; a past epoch's winner note — which is never deleted on release — has the earliest `created_at` and wins, causing the current legitimate run to return `race_lost: true`. (plugins/claim-coordinator/github-labels/plugin.sh: \"(( _CCGL_RACE_NOTE_AT > 0 )) && since=$(( _CCGL_RACE_NOTE_AT - 4 * gt ))\"; happens when: A GitHub API POST-comment response without a valid `created_at` field (partial response, proxy truncation, an rc-2 path whose response body lacks the field) while any past winner note exists on the issue — a condition that is permanent after the first successful claim on any issue.; fix: code; ACCEPTED IF: when `_CCGL_RACE_NOTE_AT` is zero the run either refuses with a backend error or uses a `since` value that provably excludes notes from prior label epochs, and a test asserts the correct behavior when the POST reply carries no `created_at`)":"RLREDTEAM-1","red-team lens, medium: If both competing runs post their notes within the same propagation window and each run's read-back executes before the other run's note is visible via the API, both runs see only their own note, both enter this branch, and both return `{\"acquired\": true}`, violating R1 (\"exactly one proceeds\"); none of the DSRACE requirements have a precondition covering this case, and the design's outside assumptions list ordering and stability of `created_at` but do not state that a comment is immediately visible to all API clients after the POST that created it. (plugins/claim-coordinator/github-labels/plugin.sh: \"if [[ -z \"$winner\" || \"$winner\" == \"$my_holder\" ]]; then\"; happens when: Two runs that add the label within milliseconds of each other on GitHub infrastructure where comment reads are served from a replica or cache that has not yet replicated the peer's newly posted note — a narrow but real condition on a busy GitHub host.; fix: design; ACCEPTED IF: the design's outside assumptions explicitly address cross-connection comment visibility consistency, or the design acknowledges the residual window where both runs proceed and states why it is acceptable)":"RLREDTEAM-2","red-team lens, medium: The ACCEPTED IF for RLREDTEAM-1 requires a test that asserts correct behavior when the POST reply carries no `created_at`; every test in claim-coordinator-unit-test.sh supplies a parseable `created_at` in post-reply.json, so the `date +%s` fallback path in `_ccgl_race_note_post_v` is never exercised, and the fail-closed guard in `claim_coordinator_claim` (`(( _CCGL_RACE_NOTE_AT == 0 ))`) is never reached by any test; a future change to the fallback logic can silently regress RLREDTEAM-1 without a test catching it. (plugins/claim-coordinator/github-labels/tests/claim-coordinator-unit-test.sh: \"printf '{\"id\":99100,\"created_at\":\"2026-01-01T00:01:00Z\",\"body\":\"\",\"user\":{\"login\":\"github-actions[bot]\"}}\\n' \\\"; happens when: A developer modifies `_ccgl_race_note_post_v`'s fallback branch (lines `_CCGL_RACE_NOTE_AT=\"$(date +%s 2>/dev/null || true)\"` and the `^[0-9]+$` guard) and the suite stays green because no test injects a no-`created_at` POST reply.; fix: test; ACCEPTED IF: a test case in claim-coordinator-unit-test.sh supplies a post-reply.json that omits `created_at` (or makes it unparseable) and asserts that the claim either proceeds with a `since` value excluding prior-epoch notes, or fails closed with `{\"acquired\": false, \"reason\": \"backend_error\"}` when `date` is also unavailable); the same kind of problem also at: plugins/claim-coordinator/github-labels/plugin.sh | _CCGL_RACE_NOTE_AT=\"$(date +%s 2>/dev/null || true)\"":"RLREDTEAM-3"}}
